In article <1992Dec16.064209.8367@eskimo.com> gregs@eskimo.com (Greg Sumner) writes:
>I've been pondering this idea too, and I came up with the same problem.
>You'd have a bunch of players starting at the same place.  It would end up
>being a race through the levels to see who could get to the good stuff
>first.  Who wants to join into a game where the first 15 levels are
>already looted anyway?

Yeah, there was that problem - the simple solution - make monster generation
occur more frequently. (Or rather, regenerate *everything* (items, etc.) more
often, depending on number of players.) That is, on each level, make the
monsters appear with a frequency based on the number of players currently on
that level. That oughta make things well-enough stocked to keep things
interesting.

Also, again assuming multiple players in the same dungeon, should their pets
start out as friendly or unfriendly toward each other? God, can you imagine
the mess with a huge dog/catfight at the start of each game...
>
>So how about this idea.  Each player has his (or her) individual game,
>just like it works now.  Except on certain levels the games can link.
>Say, on each individual game there is a portal to another player's maze,
>each joining every 8 levels or so.  Ie, there is a "common" ground
>between two games on level 8, 16, etc.  Maybe a little town or a
>gladiator ring, where players can duke it out with each other.  You could
>then enter another player's maze, but only the parts that have been
>discovered already.  Or else once two games have been linked, the new
>discoveries would have to be shared between them.  This seems much
>easier that having two players begin in the same maze, and you still get
>to meet up with other players and chat with them, or else kill them or
>kick their dog or something.
>
>I think it's Larn that begins in a town, right?  How about having a
>town with various dungeon entries that are individual from one another,
>but players would walk around together in the town, etc.

Yeah, that's not a bad idea. I was thinking about that idea as well, trying to
provide a less haphazard structure to the overall game. (But that would
probably mean putting shops on the town level, and getting rid of the fun of
raiding shops in the dungeon. Hm....)
